{
  "program": "J",
  "title": "Recompute order-dependent tie selection inside the shuffle",
  "hard": false,
  "on_path": true,
  "statement": "For 20 iid equal-count books, reselecting the first-occurring most-frequent type inside each within-block shuffle changes 18 of 20 compress p values and 19 of 20 barrier p values relative to freezing the observed stopword; neither version rejects at .05 in these samples. For 20 independent twenty-type periodic controls, both reject all runs under both gap policies. The corrected full-statistic permutation is invariant to bijective relabeling with a fixed random stream, but these limited simulations do not establish a general false-alarm rate or power.",
  "check": "Expand to independent historical and synthetic books with ties and no ties, realistic unequal frequencies, nonstationary structured controls and noisy planted ciphers. Quantify uncertainty and validate the complete order-dependent selection under the intended null; ensure blocks are independently justified and test performance under realistic exchangeability violations.",
  "method": "Generate 5,000 tokens with 20 types, each exactly 250 times. For iid controls, randomly permute the full equal-count multiset, then partition into 50 blocks of 100. For cycles, make 50 independently phased blocks of five full 20-type cycles, hence equal counts. For each book, choose the earliest occurring type among those tied for highest count, and compute the P-J-039 unequal-pair directional imbalance statistic for distances 1\u20133. Compare 99 within-block permutations using either the observed stopword for every shuffle or reselecting it independently within each shuffled book; use one-sided add-one p <= .05. Separate compression and barrier rules. Seeds and exact pseudorandom operations are in the script.",
  "limitations": "Only 20 synthetic books per condition; equal-count iid controls are exchangeable conditional on their block counts, and cycling is a highly regular alternative. The same books and shuffles are shared across fixed and reselected rules and gap policies. No historical work, manuscript text or general alternative is tested. Deterministic first-appearance tie selection is substitution-invariant but order-sensitive, which is why it is recomputed under the null.",
